# AGENTS.md — {{project_name}}

Tool-neutral agent guide for this repository. `AGENTS.md` is read by multiple
coding agents (GitHub Copilot coding agent, Claude / `claude-code-action`, Codex,
Cursor); it defines the lanes, who owns which paths, and how work is validated.
Per-lane engineering personas live in `.github/agents/<lane>.agent.md`; per-path
rules live in `.github/instructions/*.instructions.md`.

Stack: **{{stack}}**.

## Lanes

Each issue is owned by exactly one lane. An agent works only inside its lane's
paths and does not touch another lane's files.

{{lanes}}

## Path ownership

An agent may edit only the paths its lane owns. If a change seems to require
touching another lane's paths, stop and say so on the issue instead of crossing
the boundary — the dependency belongs to another issue.

### Backend

{{path_map_backend}}

### Frontend

{{path_map_frontend}}

Shared contract/type paths are owned by the backend lane and consumed read-only
by the frontend lane; the frontend never edits them.

## How work flows

1. An issue carries the full spec in its body: context, referenced requirements,
   and falsifiable acceptance criteria. Read it in full before writing code.
2. Implement on a branch, in your lane only, to the acceptance criteria.
3. Run the validation commands locally and get them green.
4. Commit and open a pull request that links the issue (`Closes #<n>`).
5. CI runs the same validation plus `suite-integrity`; a cross-model reviewer
   (`claude-review`) and Copilot review check the diff against the acceptance
   criteria. A blocking finding is returned as requested changes on the PR.

## Validation commands

These are the exact commands CI runs on every pull request. Run them locally
before opening the PR; the PR does not merge until they pass.

{{validation_commands}}

## Rules that apply to every lane

- Implement the acceptance criteria precisely and minimally — not beyond them.
- Write the error and edge paths the criteria imply before the happy path.
- Prefer early returns; keep nesting shallow (≤2 levels).
- Every acceptance criterion maps to at least one honest test that actually
  exercises the new behavior. No vacuous asserts, no tests hard-coded to pass.
- Do not add dependencies the issue does not allow; pin exact versions.
- Do not reformat unrelated files or rename for taste.
- Never edit `.github/workflows/**` to make a check pass — changing CI to go
  green is tampering and is reviewed as such.
